+### 11 · [DATA] From lamps to lifts: "One job in 270" · ~0:34 (6:46–7:20)
+**Visual:** Lưới 270 ô nhỏ, mỗi ô là biểu tượng một nghề (cách điệu, không chữ). Các ô chao nhẹ, đổi màu, co giãn, nhưng đứng nguyên. Chỉ **một** ô tắt hẳn: biểu tượng cửa thang máy có tay gạt. Chữ to: **1 in 270**. Phụ đề nguồn ở chân khung.
+**Data:** Bessen: 271 detailed occupations in the 1950 Census, followed to 2010; only elevator operators' disappearance largely attributable to automation; telegraph operators declined through technological obsolescence [24].
+
+> Lamplighting was an extreme case: a job that was almost entirely one task, and a switch could do it.
+> The economist James Bessen took about two hundred and seventy occupations from the 1950 U.S. Census and followed them to 2010.
+> Plenty disappeared, often because people stopped needing them, like telegraph operators. But only one vanished mainly because a machine took over the work: the elevator operator.
+> One in two hundred and seventy. Machines mostly took tasks, not whole jobs.
+
+### 12 · [SHORTS] "Today's shortest rounds" · ~0:47 (7:20–8:07)
+**Visual (16:9 và 9:16):** Năm cột đèn khí cách điệu xếp hàng, mỗi cột mang biểu tượng một nghề (máy đánh chữ, tai nghe tổng đài, bảng cắm dây, bàn phím, điện thoại bàn). Mỗi cột ngắn lại theo đúng tỉ lệ % (cùng thang, hổ phách nhạt dần). Chữ to trong 5 giây đầu: **−34%**. Dòng nguồn cố định: "U.S. BLS projections, 2025–35 · projected change in U.S. employment over 10 years". Không có người.
+**Data:** BLS Table 1.5, 2025–35 [25]: word processors and typists −34.4% (40.4k → 26.5k); telephone operators −27.6% (3.5k → 2.5k); switchboard operators −26.0%; data entry keyers −25.5%; telemarketers −21.4%. Office and administrative support group −4.0%; automation tools including AI cited [26].
+
+> So who is on that list today?
+> Every year, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics projects how American jobs will change over the next ten years. The newest, published in August 2026, cover 2025 to 2035.
+> Top of the fastest-shrinking list: word processors and typists, projected to fall by about thirty-four percent. Then telephone operators, down nearly twenty-eight percent; only about three and a half thousand are left. Switchboard operators and data entry keyers, each down about a quarter. Telemarketers, down about a fifth.
+> What they share: much of the work is one repeated task, and automation tools, including AI, are taking on more of it. These are forecasts, not verdicts. But the pattern sounds familiar.
+
+### 13 · [DATA] AI in the forecast · ~0:51 (8:07–8:58)
+**Visual:** Hai cột thang đo song song, cùng thang, nền tối: bên trái các nghề giảm (customer service −5.0, claims adjusters −4.4, auto damage appraisers −9.2), bên phải nghề tăng (software developers +17.9). Nhãn kỳ dự báo **2023–33** rõ ràng. Chuyển cảnh: một dải đèn hổ phách mới bật lên với biểu tượng y tá, tấm pin mặt trời, tua-bin gió, biểu đồ dữ liệu (nhãn 2025–35). Cuối đoạn: hai thanh ngang cùng thang (100 = mức lẽ ra có; 81 = mức thực tế, tức thấp hơn 19 %), nhãn "ages 22–25 · most AI-exposed jobs · U.S. payroll data to June 2026" và chữ "early signal · not proof of cause". Không vẽ người.
+**Data:** MLR Feb 2025, 2023–33 cycle [27]: customer service reps −5.0%; claims adjusters, examiners and investigators −4.4%; insurance appraisers, auto damage −9.2%; software developers +17.9%. BLS 2025–35 [26]: nurse practitioners +41.0%, solar PV installers +36.5%, data scientists +34.6%, wind turbine service technicians +29.5%; total employment +3.5%. Stanford DEL, Aug 2026, data through June 2026 [28]: ages 22–25 in highly AI-exposed occupations ~19% below counterfactual; mainly reduced hiring; no comparable gap for experienced workers; "descriptive patterns, not causal estimates".
+
+> In an earlier round, for 2023 to 2033, BLS spelled out where it expects AI to reduce demand: customer service representatives down five percent, auto damage appraisers down about nine. But software developers up almost eighteen. AI changes work; it doesn't only remove it.
+> And the newest list has fast-growing jobs too: nurse practitioners, solar installers, data scientists, wind turbine technicians, each up about thirty percent or more.
+> One early warning. Stanford's Digital Economy Lab found that, by June 2026, employment of workers aged twenty-two to twenty-five in the jobs most exposed to AI was about nineteen percent below where it would otherwise be, mostly because fewer were hired. Experienced workers showed no such gap.
+> The researchers call it an early signal, not proof that AI caused it.
+
+### 14 · [STORY + DATA] The question · ~0:33 gồm thẻ kết (8:58–9:31)
+**Visual:** Thành phố không tên lại vào chạng vạng. Đèn trắng, màn hình, cửa sổ văn phòng sáng dần; giữa chúng một ô cửa sổ hổ phách. Bốn số neo hiện lần cuối, chữ nhỏ, xếp dọc: 40,000 · 5 · 1 in 270 · −34%. Thẻ kết kênh *Last Lamplighters* · *Every era has its last lamplighters.*
+
+*IDA (off, distant):* Evening, old street.
+
+> Forty thousand lamps became about eleven hundred, and a team of five. Out of two hundred and seventy jobs, one disappeared outright. Today, some rounds are getting shorter: typists, projected down thirty-four percent.
+> Some jobs end. Most change. And new ones appear that nobody had a name for yet.
+> So, the question this channel will keep asking:
+> Who are the last lamplighters of our time?
